<p>In addition to the visuals, I can also set audio cues that alert me to different situations, but most of that already arrives via just listening. Yes, it can make errors &#8212; sometimes boxes are just toothpicks. Yes, if it&#8217;s raining, it&#8217;s a mess, but if I&#8217;m out in the rain, I&#8217;m already on high alert. Finally, it also shines a bright red on the folks behind me.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">A Bright Red Light</h2>



<p>The ride to work. Suburbia. I&#8217;m stopped at a long red light when the metal box approaches on my left. We&#8217;re both at the front and it&#8217;s clear they want to say something&#8230; the slow roll forward. The window is going down on the passenger side. They&#8217;re in driver state #2 above: something&#8217;s up.</p>



<p>&#8220;Sir. I say, sir. The light on your bike is distracting me.&#8221;</p>



<p>&lt;sfx: Deep breath></p>



<p>I turn my head and speak calmly, &#8220;My&#8230; brake light?&#8221;</p>



<p>&#8220;It&#8217;s distracting.&#8221;</p>



<p>&#8220;No, it&#8217;s not. You&#8217;ve got the same light on your car; in fact, there are two of them. I stare at them all the time. They are designed to give important information to your fellow travelers. It&#8217;s there to make sure you don&#8217;t hit me.&#8221;</p>



<p>Green light.</p>
